  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>All of the above insights are available.</b> option is correct because AWS CodeStar's dashboard is specifically designed to provide a unified view of your development project's status. This includes the commit activities from your connected CodeCommit repository, current and past statuses of each CodePipeline stage (such as source, build, and deploy), and real-time monitoring metrics and logs directly from AWS CloudWatch tailored for your Node.js app hosted on Lambda.</p><p>Why are the other options incorrect if chosen separately? <i>The full commit log from your CodeCommit repository.</i> is available, but only covers source control activity. <i>Status of your CodePipeline, including each stage like source, build, and deployment.</i> is visible through the dashboard, but doesn’t include code commits or application activity. <i>Live application activity data as reported by AWS CloudWatch.</i> is also accessible, but again, it is just one piece of the monitoring capabilities.</p><p>Tip: AWS CodeStar integrates and presents information from multiple AWS developer tools and services in one place. Always look for options that encompass the platform’s full scope when asked about dashboard monitoring or central features.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are managing a new Node.js web application that runs on AWS Lambda, and you used AWS CodeStar's templates to quickly set up the project. Within the AWS CodeStar dashboard specifically for this project, which types of information can you display and track?</p>",
